ASPIRATIONAL — tree-store TT2: a reparent that would create a CYCLE is REJECTED
by a DEFAULT-ON guard. NOT built yet.

Rulings (Lars, 2026-07-20):
  - MECHANISM: a compiler-inserted RUNTIME guard at the parent-write (walk p->root
    via parent; if n appears, or p==n, it's a cycle). NOT comptime — `p in
    subtree(n)` is runtime data. (Spec's "reuse T2 comptime" was an over-reach;
    T2 is the static interceptor graph, a different cycle.)
  - SEMANTICS: the guard mirrors insert's `| full` boundary exactly —
      * no `| cycle` handler → default @panic (loud), the pit-of-success wall.
      * `| cycle |> <action>` handler → the write is REFUSED, control goes to the
        branch, the program continues; the tree is left intact.
  - SAFETY DEFAULT: guard is ON by default. An unsafe/opt-out toggle
    (`[tree(unchecked)]`) is DEFERRED — not built now.

This pin tests the HANDLER path (deterministic stdout): the cycle write
`tree[root].parent: b` fires `| cycle` ("cycle rejected"), the write is refused
(root.parent stays -1 → root stays a root), preorder walks the intact tree.
Expected: cycle rejected / 10 / 20 / 30.

Grounded current behavior (SHOWN): without the guard the write is silently
accepted, no row keeps parent == -1, and preorder emits NOTHING.
